            "height": 496,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "radians converts angles in degrees to radians: it multiplies by pi / 180 (0.01745).\n\n180 comes out as 3.14159 (pi), 90 as 1.5708, 360 as 6.2832. A full turn is 360 degrees\nand 2 pi radians.\n\nUse it to feed an angle you think of in degrees into anything that expects radians:\nmost maths outside this patcher, the NumPy and PyTorch nodes, rotation and quaternion\nconversions.\n\nLists, NumPy arrays and PyTorch tensors go through element by element, keeping their\nshape. In the NumPy example at the bottom left, a random array (left) goes through\nradians cell by cell (right).\n\nSYNTAX:\nradians\n\nEXAMPLES:\nradians    an angle for a node that works in radians\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe data to be operated on. Receiving data here triggers the calculation.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nresult:\nThe angle in radians.\n\nSEE ALSO:\ndegrees    the other way, radians to degrees\nsin, cos   take degrees by default (a checkbox switches to radians)",
                    "value_type": "string"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 686,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 480,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "string"
                }
            },
            "text": "radians converts angles in degrees to radians: it multiplies by pi / 180 (0.01745).\n\n180 comes out as 3.14159 (pi), 90 as 1.5708, 360 as 6.2832. A full turn is 360 degrees\nand 2 pi radians.\n\nUse it to feed an angle you think of in degrees into anything that expects radians:\nmost maths outside this patcher, the NumPy and PyTorch nodes, rotation and quaternion\nconversions.\n\nLists, NumPy arrays and PyTorch tensors go through element by element, keeping their\nshape. In the NumPy example at the bottom left, a random array (left) goes through\nradians cell by cell (right).\n\nSYNTAX:\nradians\n\nEXAMPLES:\nradians    an angle for a node that works in radians\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe data to be operated on. Receiving data here triggers the calculation.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nresult:\nThe angle in radians.\n\nSEE ALSO:\ndegrees    the other way, radians to degrees\nsin, cos   take degrees by default (a checkbox switches to radians)"
        },
